  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Visual Distractions**\n\nI\'m now diving into the presentation of visual data, specifically focusing on how to minimize distractions and cognitive overload. The core recommendation from my sources aligns with the principles of DUA 3.0, emphasizing a layout strictly focused on the pedagogical action. I want to explore how to best translate this into practical design choices.\n\n\n**Refining User Interface Elements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on UI element customization. Allowing users to adjust font sizes and color contrast is a priority. I\'m also considering dividing the form into visually distinct, sequential blocks, leveraging progressive disclosure with a stepper-like approach to streamline the user experience, showing only essential questions at each stage to reduce cognitive load, to create a more efficient user interface.\n\n\n**Implementing Accessibility Features**\n\nI\'ve been thinking about enhancing accessibility features. I plan to incorporate a persistent, system-wide text size control, allowing up to 30% larger text and offering various color contrast modes: standard, high contrast (dark background, clear text), and a visual comfort mode (sepia/soft reading). I\'ll ensure the accessibility control bar is prominent, easily toggled, and incorporates shortcuts.\n\n\n**Designing User-Centric Forms**\n\nI\'m structuring the form\'s initial stages. I plan to incorporate a profile selector with visual cards for different IT education levels (Technical vs. Bachelor\'s). I\'ll also add one-click pre-filling options with example profiles from the campus, such as Lucas, Matheus, and Cau\303\243 for Technical, and Beatriz, Larissa, and \"General Case\" for Bachelor\'s. A \"Continue to Identification\" button will advance the user.\n\n\n**Structuring Form Completion Steps**\n\nI\'m now detailing the form\'s final phases. I\'m focusing on \"filtered DUA 3.0 Solutions,\" presented as a collapsible accordion or compact, selectable cards, tailored to the chosen modality. Also, I\'m integrating observed barriers in the IT lab or classroom, alongside potential interests like computational thinking. I plan to incorporate assistive technology resources and strategically placed \"Back\" and \"Review & Generate PEI\" buttons.\n\n**Summarizing with Visuals**\n\nMy focus is shifting to the summary stage. I\'ll include a concise visual card summarizing selections (Student, Modality, Discipline, Key Barriers, Selected TA, Relevant Regulation). I\'ll confirm DUA 3.0 guidelines and will offer an engaging button: \"Generate PEI with Ioda AI\" with a pedagogical animation. A stepper header will guide the process, with 4 steps labeled: Profile & Benchmark, Identification, Solutions, and Revision & Generation.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Form Steps**\n\nI\'m now implementing visual indicators for each form step: active step, completed steps with green checkmarks, and a horizontal animated progress bar. Navigation will be available through the completed steps or the footer buttons. Also, I\'m integrating DUA 3.0 accessibility features, creating an accessibility context for font size scaling (normal, enlarged, large) and contrast modes (standard, high-contrast, comfort), all globally accessible and easily toggled via an accessibility bar in the navbar.\n\n\n"
